{
  "dom_id": "step-q23996",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2)=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "By the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘}$  are $x={0}^{∘},{180}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2.$ But since $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2$ has no solution.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={0}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0\\cdot 180=0.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
